.TH HSH 1 "6 September 2026" "Holberton School" "Simple Shell Manual"

.SH NAME
hsh \- a simple UNIX command interpreter

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh
.RI [ options ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple UNIX command interpreter written in C as part of the
Holberton School Simple Shell project.

The shell reads commands from standard input, separates the input into
a command and its arguments, searches for executable files using the
PATH environment variable, and executes commands using
.BR fork (2)
and
.BR execve (2).

The shell can be used interactively from a terminal or non-interactively
with commands provided through standard input.

When running interactively, the shell displays a prompt containing the
current working directory.

.SH INVOCATION

To start the shell interactively:

.EX
$ ./hsh
.EE

Commands may then be entered at the prompt.

The shell can also receive commands through standard input:

.EX
$ echo "ls -l" | ./hsh
.EE

or:

.EX
$ printf "pwd\\nls\\n" | ./hsh
.EE

.SH COMMAND EXECUTION

If the command contains a slash, the shell attempts to execute the given
path directly.

For example:

.EX
/bin/ls
./program
.EE

If the command does not contain a slash, the shell searches each directory
listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable until an executable file with the requested name is
found.

For example:

.EX
ls
pwd
echo Hello
.EE

Commands may be followed by arguments separated by spaces or tab
characters.

.SH BUILTINS

The following built-in commands are currently supported.

.TP
.B exit
Exits the shell.

The shell returns the status of the most recently executed command.

.TP
.B env
Prints the current environment variables.

.TP
.B cd [directory]
Changes the current working directory.

If
.I directory
is provided, the shell attempts to change to that directory.

.EX
cd /tmp
cd ..
.EE

If
.B cd
is used without an argument, the shell attempts to change to the directory
specified by the
.B HOME
environment variable.

.EX
cd
.EE

If
.B cd -
is used, the shell attempts to change to the directory specified by the
.B OLDPWD
environment variable.

.EX
cd -
.EE

.TP
.B clear
Clears the interactive terminal display.

.TP
.B debug
Toggles the shell's debug output while the shell is running.

Debug output includes information about input parsing, command lookup,
arguments, and child-process exit status.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.B -ss
Starts the shell without displaying the interactive startup banner.

.TP
.B -debug
Starts the shell with debug output enabled.

.SH INTERACTIVE MODE

When standard input is connected to a terminal,
.B hsh
operates in interactive mode.

The shell displays a prompt containing the current working directory and
waits for commands from the user.

For example:

.EX
/home/user/project$> ls
.EE

The startup banner and interactive prompt are not displayed when standard
input comes from a pipe or redirected input.

.SH NON-INTERACTIVE MODE

When standard input is not connected to a terminal, commands are read and
executed without displaying the interactive prompt or startup banner.

Example:

.EX
$ echo "/bin/pwd" | ./hsh
/home/user/project
.EE

Multiple commands may be supplied on separate lines:

.EX
$ printf "/bin/pwd\\n/bin/ls\\n" | ./hsh
.EE

.SH ENVIRONMENT

.TP
.B PATH
Contains the directories searched when a command is entered without an
explicit path.

If
.B PATH
is not present, commands containing explicit paths may still be executed.

.TP
.B HOME
Used as the destination when
.B cd
is executed without an argument.

.TP
.B OLDPWD
Used as the destination when
.B cd -
is executed.

.SH EXIT STATUS

For external commands,
.B hsh
waits for the child process and records its exit status.

A successfully executed command normally returns the exit status supplied
by that command.

If a command cannot be located,
.B hsh
sets the command status to 127.

.SH EXAMPLES

Start the shell:

.EX
$ ./hsh
.EE

Execute a command using PATH:

.EX
$> ls -l
.EE

Execute a command using an absolute path:

.EX
$> /bin/ls -l
.EE

Print environment variables:

.EX
$> env
.EE

Change directory:

.EX
$> cd /tmp
.EE

Change to HOME:

.EX
$> cd
.EE

Execute commands non-interactively:

.EX
$ echo "ls -l" | ./hsh
.EE

Exit the shell:

.EX
$> exit
.EE

.SH LIMITATIONS

.B hsh
is an educational simple shell and does not implement all features of
.BR sh (1)
or
.BR bash (1).

Current limitations include:

.IP \(bu 2
No command pipelines using the pipe operator.

.IP \(bu 2
No input or output redirection.

.IP \(bu 2
No command separators such as semicolons.

.IP \(bu 2
No logical operators such as && or ||.

.IP \(bu 2
No shell variable expansion.

.IP \(bu 2
No wildcard expansion.

.IP \(bu 2
No quoting or advanced shell parsing.

.IP \(bu 2
PWD and OLDPWD environment maintenance is limited.

.SH COMPILATION

The shell may be compiled using:

.EX
gcc -Wall -Werror -Wextra -pedantic -std=gnu89 *.c -o hsh
.EE

.SH FILES

.TP
.B Shell.c
Contains the main shell loop, built-in dispatch, PATH lookup, and command
execution control.

.TP
.B Functions.c
Contains helper functions for tokenization, prompting, directory changes,
process execution, and PATH handling.

.TP
.B main.h
Contains required headers and function prototypes.

